1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,070
Listen to part of a radio program about nature.

2
00:00:03,070 --> 00:00:09,030
The camel is an example of an animal that is especially adapted to survive in harsh conditions.

3
00:00:09,030 --> 00:00:11,290
First of all，camels have humps on their backs.

4
00:00:11,290 --> 00:00:17,220
Now many people think that camels hold water in their humps.But that is actually not true.

5
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:23,140
While camels do have to store water, what they carry in their humps is not water but fat.

6
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:27,290
That’s their emergency supply of energy in case there’s little food to eat.

7
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:31,736
Of course camels wouldn’t need to carry a safety supply of fat if it

8
00:00:31,736 --> 00:00:34,070
were easy to find something to eat where they live.

9
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:41,010
Unfortunately in a desert, camels can sometimes walk for days in the hot sun looking for food and water.

10
00:00:41,010 --> 00:00:44,625
So when there is little to eat，the fat from their hump allows them to

11
00:00:44,625 --> 00:00:48,120
survive for quite a long time, even up to two weeks without eating anything.

12
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:53,280
When they do get an opportunity to eat and drink, camels consume as much as possible.

13
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:58,712
A camel can drink incredible amounts of water，a lot more than most other animals

14
00:00:58,712 --> 00:01:00,100
can take in without getting sick.

15
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:03,200
You see, there’s a limit on how much an animal can drink.

16
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:06,321
If an animal were to drink more than necessary,

17
00:01:06,321 --> 00:01:09,120
that could even be bad for that animal’s health.

18
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:13,090
But a camel’s body does not suffer the effects of too much water.

19
00:01:13,090 --> 00:01:17,013
After it has stored up plenty of food and water, it can go on

20
00:01:17,013 --> 00:01:18,260
for days in a hot desert.
